 ## Background
-The concept of masking losses to exclude invalid background rays is standard in differentiable volume rendering to prevent biased gradient updates from empty regions [S1]. Area loss scaling was introduced in Mip-NeRF to handle multiscale datasets where low-resolution images have larger pixel footprints [S1]. Density-based losses for shape editing were proposed in Editing Conditional Radiance Fields to facilitate faster object manipulation [S2]. Bayer masking was introduced in RawNeRF to correctly supervise neural radiance fields using raw sensor data, avoiding artifacts from demosaicking [S3]. Edge loss regularization was added in NeRF Analogies to preserve high-frequency details during appearance transfer [S4].
+Area loss scaling was introduced in Mip-NeRF to handle multiscale datasets where low-resolution images have larger pixel footprints [S1]. Density-based losses for shape editing were proposed in Editing Conditional Radiance Fields to facilitate faster object manipulation [S2]. Bayer masking was introduced in RawNeRF to correctly supervise neural radiance fields using raw sensor data, avoiding artifacts from demosaicking [S3]. Edge loss regularization was added in NeRF Analogies to preserve high-frequency details during appearance transfer [S4]. Masked Volume Metrics was developed to exclude empty froxels from evaluation metrics [S1]. Training Loss Computation with Huber and Masking formalized the pipeline for differentiable volume rendering using Huber loss and validity-based masking [S1].
 
